---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Data" description="Where country-kit data comes from, what is official, and what we deliberately omit.">
  <section class="container prose prose-stone max-w-3xl py-12">
    <h1>Data sources</h1>
    <p>
      country-kit only ships identifiers that map to a published standard. Flags are the exception: ISO 3166 does not define flag artwork, so we use the public Wikipedia/Wikimedia SVGs.
    </p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Field</th>
          <th>Authority</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>code</code>, <code>alpha3</code>, <code>name</code></td>
          <td>ISO 3166-1 assigned English short names (249 codes)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>numeric</code>, <code>region</code>, <code>subregion</code></td>
          <td>UN M49 (numeric codes are identical to ISO 3166-1 numeric)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>callingCode</code></td>
          <td>ITU-T E.164 country calling codes (1–3 digits)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>nanpAreaCodes</code> / <code>dialCode</code></td>
          <td>NANP NPAs for phone UIs; not E.164 country codes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>independent</code></td>
          <td>ISO 3166 independent-territory flag (195 yes)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>tld</code></td>
          <td>IANA ccTLD. <code>GB</code> is <code>.uk</code>; <code>.gb</code> is unused</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>currencies</code></td>
          <td>ISO 4217 alphabetic codes. Empty for Antarctica and Palestine (no assigned code)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>capital</code></td>
          <td>Conventional English capital (not an ISO field)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>flag</code> emoji</td>
          <td>Unicode UTS #51 regional indicator symbols</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>SVG flags</td>
          <td>
            <a href="https://github.com/lipis/flag-icons">flag-icons</a> 4×3 and 1×1 SVGs (MIT), Wikipedia/Wikimedia originals, pinned at v7.5.0
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Calling codes vs dial prefixes</h2>
    <p>
      Every NANP member’s E.164 <em>country</em> code is <code>+1</code>. Area codes such as Anguilla <code>264</code> are national destination codes. country-kit stores them on <code>nanpAreaCodes</code> and concatenates the primary NPA onto <code>dialCode</code> so a phone picker can still show <code>+1264</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Saint Helena, Ascension and Tristan da Cunha (<code>SH</code>) is one ISO entry with two E.164 codes: <code>+290</code> and <code>+247</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>TLD exceptions</h2>
    <p>
      Almost every assigned alpha-2 code maps to <code>.</code> plus the lowercase code. The documented IANA exception in this dataset is the United Kingdom: <code>GB</code> → <code>.uk</code>. Saint Barthélemy is <code>.bl</code> and Saint Martin (French) is <code>.mf</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>What we deliberately omit</h2>
    <p>
      These are useful, but they are other standards or large datasets. They stay out of the default bundle on purpose:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Kosovo (<code>XK</code>)</strong> — user-assigned, not ISO 3166-1 assigned. A future opt-in such as <code>{`{ includeUserAssigned: true }`}</code> would fit.</li>
      <li><strong>ISO 3166-2 subdivisions</strong> — thousands of rows; better as <code>country-kit/subdivisions</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>CLDR localized names</strong> — keep English official + common names in core; add a locale pack later.</li>
      <li><strong>Timezones, languages, GeoJSON</strong> — IANA tz, ISO 639, and geometry are different products.</li>
      <li><strong>CI freshness against Debian <code>iso-codes</code></strong> — a generator check, not a runtime feature.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Regenerate</h2>
    <pre><code>pnpm --filter country-kit generate        # ISO / UN / extras
pnpm --filter country-kit generate:flags  # Wikipedia SVGs from flag-icons</code></pre>
    <p>
      Sources live in <code>packages/country-kit/scripts/sources/</code>. The generator refuses duplicate TLDs, unknown ISO extras, and currency codes that are not three letters.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
